WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.500
Good morning, John.

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.620
As you know, I spent the first twenty-two years of my life

00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:07.760
living in Florida, where there are 43 million bugs per square centimeter

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.500
and the winter is the best time of the year because you can move around without getting sweaty.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:14.800
And then, in 2003, I moved to Missoula, Montana, which was a shift.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:17.180
Usually hovers around the twenties and thirties (-7° to -1°C) in the winter.

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:20.620
Sometimes it gets down to like a negative twenty (-29°C). That's about as cold as it gets.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:25.520
And there are lots of people who told me the secrets to living in a cold place.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:27.660
And their secrets weren't secret!

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:30.500
Everybody told me it's layers, the secret is layers.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:33.860
Like, oh, I never would have guessed that wearing more clothes would help.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:37.360
But nobody said the secret is don't touch metal with your bare hands

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.780
because they were like, "Why would anyone do that?"

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:41.800
I'm from Florida; pretend I'm a baby.

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:44.780
So, from person who has made a successful transition

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:50.200
from living in a warm climate to loving cold weather, here are the actual secrets.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.600
Thirty degrees (-1°C) is not cold.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:56.760
I'm talkin' Fahrenheit here. Thirty degrees (-1°C) is at most chilly?

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:58.980
The difference between sixty (16°C) and thirty (-1°C).

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.200
I knew that that was a big difference.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.420
What I did not understand is the difference between thirty (-1°C) and zero (-18°C)

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:10.140
is the same size! I just thought it was all: It got cold and then it was cold forever! No, uh.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:16.080
the difference between twenty (-7°C) and negative twenty (-29°C) is very big like, life-threateningly big.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:20.840
Cold begins when it is physically painful to have exposed skin.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:24.720
Very cold begins around zero degrees Fahrenheit (-18°C), and then below Very Cold

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:29.700
we have what's called Too Cold, which is a temperature that you should not go out in

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.960
unless you have become an expert in existing at that temperature.

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:37.500
Number two: Walking on ice is something that you just get better at as you try to do it more,

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:40.240
but it is very dangerous.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:43.700
These are called traction cleats. They're sometimes also called crampons,

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:48.260
even though crampons are technically a separate thing which are used for climbing on ice and,

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:50.360
I assume, also murder.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:52.920
They're not cheap, but they're cheaper than a broken arm.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:55.400
If you know it's gonna snow, pull your wipers up,

00:01:55.400 --> 00:02:00.200
so that they don't freeze to the windshield and then break when you try to chip 'em off.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:06.540
Gloves are not for making your hands warm once they are cold. This was very confusing to me.

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:11.020
Gloves keep your hands warm. Put them on before your hands get cold.

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:15.980
Also, this is a fun tip. If you put your glove on before you put your jacket on,

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:20.040
it makes a seal between the glove and the jacket. It's all about having good seals.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:23.060
That's why I never understood scarves! I didn't know what scarves were for!

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:27.860
This is a scarf, this is the easiest way to put on a scarf. There's like a thousand million others,

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:31.180
but scarves are there to create the seal between your jacket and your neck.

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:33.040
Also, you can sometimes pull them up and do one of these.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:36.180
Don't lick your lips a bunch? That's a disaster.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:39.480
My big cold jacket? 100% of the time, there's a chapstick in the pocket.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.960
December is the beginning of winter.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:47.600
If you're in the late January, where we are right now, and you think that winter is almost over, you are going to be

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.240
very upset when April rolls around.

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:53.680
Sunlight is important. If it's sunny out and there's no wind,

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:56.500
it could be, like, T-shirt weather in the thirties (0°C-4°C).

00:02:56.500 --> 00:03:01.200
Also, seeing the sun and feeling it on your skin makes you much less miserable.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.680
Wind is much more important than sun.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:06.980
If it's - If it's in the teens (-11°C to -7°C) and windy, just give up.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:10.560
I'm not going to talk a bunch about driving except to say: It's different, go slow.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.140
And also, you need one of these.

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:16.040
They're extremely inexpensive and you should probably buy two, because if it breaks, then suddenly

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:21.580
you have a $15,000 piece of equipment that you cannot operate because of the lack of a $4 piece of equipment.

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:24.340
Also, don't buy those super short ones, I don't even know why they sell those.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:28.040
I almost forgot an important one! Do not turn your heater off if you go on vacation.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:30.160
Your pipes will explode, and your house will be destroyed.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:34.800
Likewise, don't accidentally leave a gallon of milk in the back of the car... Yep.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.460
And finally, try to enjoy it!

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:41.020
It's very pretty. It's a little refreshing, it feels very clean.

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:43.480
The sun sometimes comes out and that's nice.

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:46.600
Even if it's just a walk in the park, appreciate it!

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:52.260
If only like a teeny teeny bit, because otherwise, you will resent it... a lot.

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:54.160
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

